WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:04.870
Over the course of more than two dozen appearances
on the Adult Swim series Tim and Eric Awesome

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:10.219
Show, Great Job!, John C. Reilly bewildered
and delighted audiences as the unforgettable

00:00:10.219 --> 00:00:16.059
Dr. Steve Brule, who offered viewers life
tips in a segment called "Brule's Rules."

00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:18.800
"I'm Doctor Steve Brule, with another Brule
Rule for ya.

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:22.560
If you're raking the leaves and it gets all
over your driveway, just hose it off, dummy!"

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:28.200
The character proved so popular that Tim Heidecker,
Eric Wareheim, and Reilly spun off the character

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:31.620
into his own series, Check it Out! with Dr.
Steve Brule.

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:34.580
Here's just about everything there is to know
about Dr. Brule.

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:36.020
"Check it out!"

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:37.980
Birth of a dingus

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:44.300
In early 2006, this unlikely trio of collaborators
met at a bowling alley during a birthday party

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:48.900
for comedian and actor Ron Lynch, who'd appeared
in Tim and Eric's cult hit cartoon show Tom

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:50.320
Goes to the Mayor.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:55.720
At that time, John C. Reilly was already a
well-respected character actor who'd even

00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:57.740
earned an Oscar nomination for Chicago.

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:02.920
The three hit it off, and Heidecker and Wareheim
invited Reilly to be on their show.

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:06.920
Reilly accepted the invitation, playing a
crazed version of himself that Tom meets on

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:10.140
a social networking site called Friendship
Alliance.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:11.060
"Tom!

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:11.920
No!

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:12.740
No!

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:13.360
No!

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:13.920
No!

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:14.500
No!

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:15.000
No!

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:15.560
No!

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:16.080
NO!"

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:20.380
Tom Goes to the Mayor wrapped up a few months
later, and Tim and Eric moved on to their

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:20.880
next show.

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:23.540
Reilly arrived with costume and character
in tow.

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:25.720
Eric Wareheim later told Reddit:

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:27.580
"Brule was John's idea completely.

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:32.850
He improvises most if not all of what he does—he
even brought his own outfit (along with the

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:33.820
hair) to the show."

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:37.140
Heidecker and Wareheim just set Reilly free
to do what he wanted.

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:40.000
"In 1971 Bill Grates invented Michaelsoft.

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:44.500
Wouldn't it be cool if I could remember my
dingus password for my email?

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:46.480
That would be cool!"

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:48.560
Brule is real

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:53.820
We know that Dr. Steve Brule is portrayed
by John C. Reilly…or is he?

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:55.960
"John, that's clearly you."

00:01:57.020 --> 00:01:59.380
"Nah, he was wearing glasses, Conan."

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:03.680
Reilly has insisted that his relationship
to Brule is as executive producer of Check

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:07.220
It Out!, and that he's never even met the
doctor in person.

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:10.660
"Are you trying you trying to say that you're
not Doctor Steve Brule, that Doctor Steve

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:11.720
Brule isn't you?"

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:12.860
"No, he's a DOCTOR."

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:17.180
However, Reilly will occasionally let the
veil slip: he told Esquire that he didn't

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:21.800
really create the character as much as he
just showed up for an Awesome Show taping,

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:22.760
saying he:

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:23.760
"...started channeling that guy.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:25.240
I don't know where he came from."

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:29.540
"I think of him as real, and anytime I start
to analyze him, it gets really boring."

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:31.780
"Some things are just better left mysterious.

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:34.040
I'm not really interested in picking it apart."

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:36.760
"Got ya.

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:37.680
Check it out."

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:39.960
Doctor to doctor

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:42.980
The Brule experience is more than just John
C. Reilly.

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:48.600
On Check It Out!, he interviews business owners,
craftspeople, athletes, artists, and more.

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:51.120
If they seem uncomfortable or flummoxed by
Dr. Steve, they're not faking it.

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:55.360
Tim Heidecker told Entertainment Weekly that
they're all completely real, saying

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:58.560
"...the comedy is not necessarily about fooling
these people.

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:02.740
It's more the character of Steve Brule being
completely clueless and a really strange guy."

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:05.700
"You have pretty hair like a lady!"

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:06.800
"Thank you!"

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:13.900
Tim mirrored the same sentiments that Eric
expressed in his Reddit AMA: they don't want

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:17.840
to make the participants the butt of the joke,
but they want their genuine reactions.

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:23.040
The confusion of the guests is heightened,
Reilly says, by the show's lack of "pre-interviews."

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:27.240
When viewers see Brule talk to people, they're
really encountering one another for the first

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:27.760
time.

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:29.700
For Denny's sake

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:31.760
"... watching the hands come out."

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:33.680
"Well, all this talk about chips is making
me hungry.

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:35.220
Denny, give me some chips!"

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:39.180
Throughout Check it Out!, Dr. Brule barks
at a guy known only as Denny.

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:42.400
"Denny, you're a dirtbag, mugger guy.

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:46.360
And you're gonna come, and I'm gonna keep
you off of my case, man."

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:50.900
According to Reilly, Denny's the person that
"does the technicals"—he's responsible for

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:52.780
the sound, camerawork, and editing.

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:57.660
The reason he doesn't seem to be particularly
good at any of it is because Denny "has a

00:03:57.660 --> 00:03:58.420
lot of jobs."

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:04.940
"Here's ol' Denny, he has a workshop making
all of these DVDs for you dingos."

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:07.740
Another one of Denny's jobs is being Steve's
handler.

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:12.840
Furthering the illusion that Dr. Steve and
John C. Reilly aren't the same person, Reilly

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:16.320
and his collaborators say they get in touch
with Brule by paging Denny.

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:20.800
Denny then faxes Steve, and then Steve sends
a fax back to Denny.

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:22.220
Bagboy

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:28.979
In 2015, Adult Swim aired a Dr. Steve Brule
special called Bagboy, presented as a sitcom

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:33.300
pilot created by and starring Brule as an
inept grocery store worker.

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:37.460
It wasn't really written by Brule, of course,
but by Tim, Eric, and Reilly.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:43.640
Before they began, Heidecker researched real
supermarket-set sitcoms to nail down the characteristics.

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:49.200
As it turns out, there haven't been too many,
but the first and most prominent one Heidecker

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:53.620
could find was a 1980s Canadian sitcom starring
Don Adams.

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:54.120
Its title?

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:04.300
Bagboy was based heavily on 1980's Check It
Out, down to the attractive checkout girl

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:08.400
and the inept security guard, and the rest
is very weird history.

00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:10.220
For your health

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:12.900
Doctor Brule is no stranger to monkeys…

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:14.700
"What kind of animal you want to be, dingus?"

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:16.660
"Probably a dog."

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:17.180
"Nope."

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:18.840
"No?"

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:19.520
"That's not it.

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:21.040
You'd be a dangatang.

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:22.440
That's what you'd look like."

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:28.000
...and in the 2017 blockbuster Kong: Skull
Island, Reilly plays an Air Force lieutenant

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:31.760
named Marlow, who's been stranded on
Skull Island for three decades.

00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:37.020
The movie also features what's got to be an
allusion to Dr. Steve Brule: Reilly's character

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:41.860
wears his old military jacket, which displays
the name of his squadron along with the words

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:43.120
"Good for your health."

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:45.160
That can't be a coincidence, right?

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:46.640
"For your health!"

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:47.320
"For your health!"

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:48.800
"For your health!"

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:51.960
Brule's rules

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:56.539
There's one big, lingering question about
Dr. Steve Brule: how does this guy have a

00:05:56.540 --> 00:05:57.340
doctorate in anything?

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:00.320
"I don't have to say anything.

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:02.220
I'm a doctor too."

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:06.380
According to Reilly's Reddit interview, Brule
just might not be a doctor at all.

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:10.620
He revealed that his mother may have actually
named him Doctor Steven Brule.

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:13.340
It's wouldn't be the weirdest thing his mother
did to him…

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:18.240
"I got locked in a basement.

00:06:19.420 --> 00:06:21.520
Mommy fed me raisins."

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:24.400
"And that's all she wrote!

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:27.960
I'm Doctor Steve Brule, for your wine."

00:06:28.789 --> 00:06:29.780
Thanks for watching!

00:06:29.789 --> 00:06:32.830
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:32.830 --> 00:06:35.659
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

